WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.691
A payment service has three threads, each handling one card authorization.

2
00:00:07.691 --> 00:00:09.647
The machine has one CPU core.

3
00:00:09.867 --> 00:00:17.596
A core runs one thread at a time, whatever sits in its registers: the next line of code, the stack, and the values in use.

4
00:00:17.733 --> 00:00:23.180
Every few milliseconds a timer interrupts, and the kernel's scheduler picks who runs next.

5
00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:27.060
It saves T1's registers to memory and loads T2's.

6
00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:28.853
That's a context switch.

7
00:00:29.067 --> 00:00:33.575
With turns of about 3 milliseconds each, all three seem to run at once.

8
00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:39.441
Each switch costs a trip into the kernel, and leaves caches full of another thread's data.

9
00:00:39.441 --> 00:00:44.331
With 30 busy threads on one core, each waits about 90 milliseconds for a turn.

10
00:00:44.533 --> 00:00:47.182
But most service threads just wait.

11
00:00:47.182 --> 00:00:51.117
T2 calls the card issuer and waits 120 milliseconds.

12
00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:56.131
While it waits, it's off the queue, so the core runs only T1 and T3.

13
00:00:56.131 --> 00:00:59.185
When the reply comes, T2 is runnable again.

14
00:00:59.367 --> 00:01:02.708
So a waiting service wants a thread per request.

15
00:01:02.708 --> 00:01:09.321
10,000 requests in flight means 10,000 platform threads, each a kernel task with its own stack.

16
00:01:09.533 --> 00:01:17.455
That's 10,000 tasks for the kernel to switch between, and about 20 gigabytes of stack reserved, almost all of it for waiting.

17
00:01:17.633 --> 00:01:20.977
Virtual threads move that switching into the JDK.

18
00:01:20.977 --> 00:01:23.569
Each one is a Java object on the heap.

19
00:01:23.569 --> 00:01:27.936
To run, it mounts on a carrier, a platform thread, one per core.

20
00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:33.252
When one blocks on the issuer, the JDK saves its stack on the heap and unmounts it.

21
00:01:33.252 --> 00:01:37.287
The carrier picks up the next one, and the kernel sees no switch.

22
00:01:37.467 --> 00:01:47.884
In the lab, under a limit of 64 tasks, platform threads failed after 42, while 10,000 virtual threads ran on 37 OS threads.

23
00:01:48.067 --> 00:01:52.101
A core runs one thread at a time, and every kernel switch costs.

24
00:01:52.101 --> 00:01:57.019
Virtual threads make waiting cheap, so thousands can wait on a few OS threads.
